<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- "Animation is a pure function of time, not a mutation." That is a statement about
         values, and it needs nothing but `Marey.Core`: an `Anim<'a>` is generic in what it
         animates, so this project never mentions a Figure, a Scene or a backend.

         netstandard2.1 like every other library here, and this one *is* Fable-compatible and
         should stay that way: a scrubbable timeline in a browser is the single most obvious
         thing to build on this type, and it is exactly what Manim's mutable `Scene` cannot do.

         NOTHING may be added to <PackageReference> in this file. The interpolation witness it
         needs is Core's, and Core has no dependencies to inherit. -->
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.1</TargetFramework>
    <!-- Packed with its sources under fable/, so a Fable app can reference the
         package (Directory.Build.targets). -->
    <MareyFable>true</MareyFable>
    <AssemblyName>Marey.Anim</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Marey</RootNamespace>
    <Description>Animation as a pure function of time for Marey: a seekable, composable Anim&lt;'a&gt; whose algebra is property-tested, the easing catalogue, and morphing between paths along their arc length. Depends only on Marey.Core.</Description>
    <PackageTags>fsharp visualization plotting animation easing tweening scientific</PackageTags>

    <!-- A package ships with a producer: one nothing else can consume is a package a reader is
         asked to install for nothing. This one's producer is `Chart.toSceneIn` with
         `Chart.arrangeUnion`: one `Anim.atFraction` per frame, into an arrangement sized for the
         whole shot.

         **The producer is in `Marey.Plot`, not here, and that is deliberate.** It has to name
         `Figure`, `Arrangement` and `ITextMeasurer`, and this project mentions none of them by
         design. Referencing `Marey.Plot` from here would break that; referencing this from
         `Marey.Plot` would make every static-only consumer of the main package carry an
         animation assembly. So the union takes a `float -> Figure` sampler and lives where
         `Arrangement` lives. -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Compile order is the layer order. `Ease` is a catalogue of `float -> float` and knows
         nothing about `Anim`; `Anim.ease` takes any such function, so the dependency runs one
         way and this order is the one that says so. `Morph` is last because it is the only file
         here that knows what a `PathSeg` is, and because `Morph.anim` returns one. -->
    <Compile Include="Ease.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Anim.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Morph.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Marey.Core\Marey.Core.fsproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
